Certainly! Below is a simple Chrome extension ("plugin") that, when you click its button, will **save the contents of the current page** (the HTML) as a `.html` file.

**Directory structure:**
```
save-page/
├── manifest.json
├── background.js
└── icon.png   (any 128x128 icon)
```

---

### 1. `manifest.json`
```json
{
  "manifest_version": 3,
  "name": "Save Page As HTML",
  "version": "1.0",
  "description": "Save the current page as an HTML file.",
  "permissions": ["scripting", "downloads", "activeTab"],
  "background": {
    "service_worker": "background.js"
  },
  "action": {
    "default_title": "Save Page"
  },
  "icons": {
    "128": "icon.png"
  }
}
```

---

### 2. `background.js`
```js
chrome.action.onClicked.addListener(async (tab) => {
  // Execute script to get the page's HTML content
  const [result] = await chrome.scripting.executeScript({
    target: { tabId: tab.id },
    func: () => {
      // Get current HTML content
      return document.documentElement.outerHTML;
    }
  });

  const htmlContent = result.result;
  const filename = (tab.title || "page").replace(/[\\/:*?"<>|]+/g, "_") + ".html";

  // Create a blob and download
  const url = URL.createObjectURL(new Blob([htmlContent], {type: 'text/html'}));
  chrome.downloads.download({
    url: url,
    filename: filename,
    saveAs: true
  }, () => {
    URL.revokeObjectURL(url);
  });
});
```

---

### 3. `icon.png`
Use any 128x128 PNG icon. (You can use a placeholder or draw one.)

---

## How it works

- When you click the extension's icon, it grabs the full HTML of the page.
- It then triggers a download of that HTML as a file named after the page's title.
- It uses `chrome.scripting.executeScript` (MV3 compatible).

---

## Installation

1. Put the above files in a folder (`save-page`).
2. Go to `chrome://extensions/`, enable "Developer mode".
3. Click "Load unpacked", select your `save-page` folder.
4. Pin and click the extension icon to save the page!

---

**Note:**  
- This saves the static HTML only (not images, CSS, etc).  
- For a more advanced "complete" save, more complex logic is needed.  
- This works on most pages, but not on special Chrome pages or some protected sites.

Let me know if you want to save the page as PDF or as "webpage, complete" instead!

