Sure! You can create a simple chat app in **vanilla JavaScript** using [`transformers.js`](https://xenova.github.io/transformers.js/) and the `facebook/blenderbot-400M-distill` model.

Below, I’ll guide you through the process:

---

## 1. **Include the Transformers.js Library**

Add this to your HTML:

```html
<script type="module">
  import { pipeline } from "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@xenova/transformers@2.14.0/dist/transformers.min.js";
  window.pipeline = pipeline;
</script>
```

> **Note:** You must use a server (not `file://`) or use [Live Server](https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=ritwickdey.LiveServer) in VSCode.

---

## 2. **HTML Structure**

Here's a minimal chat interface:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>BlenderBot 400M JS Chat</title>
  <style>
    body { font-family: Arial, sans-serif; max-width: 400px; margin: 40px auto; }
    #messages { border: 1px solid #ccc; height: 300px; overflow-y: auto; padding: 10px; }
    .user { color: blue; }
    .bot  { color: green; }
    input, button { font-size: 1em; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <h2>BlenderBot 400M Chat</h2>
  <div id="messages"></div>
  <form id="chat-form">
    <input id="user-input" autocomplete="off" placeholder="Say something..." required />
    <button type="submit">Send</button>
  </form>
  <script type="module" src="chat.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```

---

## 3. **JavaScript: Loading and Using BlenderBot**

Create `chat.js` (referenced in the HTML above):

```javascript
import { pipeline } from "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@xenova/transformers@2.14.0/dist/transformers.min.js";

const messagesDiv = document.getElementById('messages');
const chatForm = document.getElementById('chat-form');
const userInput = document.getElementById('user-input');

let chatbot;

// Load model
async function loadModel() {
  messagesDiv.innerHTML += `<div>Loading model (first time can take up to a minute)...</div>`;
  chatbot = await pipeline('conversational', 'Xenova/blenderbot-400M-distill');
  messagesDiv.innerHTML += `<div>Model loaded! Say hi 👋</div>`;
}
loadModel();

function appendMessage(text, sender) {
  const div = document.createElement('div');
  div.className = sender;
  div.textContent = sender === 'user' ? 'You: ' + text : 'Bot: ' + text;
  messagesDiv.appendChild(div);
  messagesDiv.scrollTop = messagesDiv.scrollHeight;
}

let conversation = []; // To keep context

chatForm.addEventListener('submit', async (e) => {
  e.preventDefault();
  const text = userInput.value.trim();
  if (!text || !chatbot) return;
  appendMessage(text, 'user');
  userInput.value = '';
  // Add user message to context
  conversation.push({ role: 'user', content: text });
  appendMessage('...', 'bot'); // Placeholder
  // Generate response
  const response = await chatbot(conversation.map(msg => msg.content).join('\n'));
  // Remove placeholder
  messagesDiv.removeChild(messagesDiv.lastChild);
  appendMessage(response.generated_text, 'bot');
  // Add bot response to context
  conversation.push({ role: 'bot', content: response.generated_text });
});
```

---

## 4. **How it Works**

- When you send a message, it:
  1. Appends your message.
  2. Passes the conversation history to the model (as context).
  3. Appends the bot's response.

---

## 5. **Notes**

- On **first load**, the model will download (~hundreds of MB), so it can take up to a minute.
- The context is tracked in the `conversation` array.
- This is **pure vanilla JS**; no frameworks.

---

## 6. **Live Demo**

You can try this by saving `index.html` and `chat.js` and running via Live Server or any static server.

---

