Certainly! Here’s a piece of Java code that uses `Stream` to process a `List<T>`, and behaves as you described:

- If the list has **no entries**, return `null`
- If **exactly one entry**, return that entry
- If **more than one**, throw an exception

```java
import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.*;

public class SingleOrNull {
    public static <T> T getSingleOrNull(List<T> list) {
        Iterator<T> iterator = list.stream().iterator();
        if (!iterator.hasNext()) {
            // No elements
            return null;
        }
        T first = iterator.next();
        if (iterator.hasNext()) {
            // More than one element
            throw new IllegalStateException("List has more than one element");
        }
        return first; // Exactly one
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> empty = Collections.emptyList();
        List<String> single = Collections.singletonList("Hello");
        List<String> multiple = Arrays.asList("A", "B");

        System.out.println(getSingleOrNull(empty));      // null
        System.out.println(getSingleOrNull(single));     // Hello
        System.out.println(getSingleOrNull(multiple));   // throws exception
    }
}
```

**Note:**  
- This code uses `stream().iterator()` to traverse the stream only as much as necessary, so it’s efficient.
- If you want a pure stream solution, you can do:

```java
public static <T> T getSingleOrNull(List<T> list) {
    List<T> results = list.stream().limit(2).collect(Collectors.toList());
    if (results.isEmpty()) return null;
    if (results.size() > 1) throw new IllegalStateException("List has more than one element");
    return results.get(0);
}
```

Both variants work well!  
