Great! Let's build out the **FavoritesManager** and **FavoritesController** as described. Here’s a best-practice Laravel approach that keeps things clean and testable.

---

## 1. FavoritesManager class

Handles favorites for both **authenticated users** (persist to DB) and **guests** (store in session).

Assumptions:

- `Favorite` model exists and relates a `user_id` to a `business_name` (or similar identifier).
- For guests, we'll store an array of business names in the session under, e.g., `'favorites'`.

```php
<?php

namespace App\Favorites;

use App\Models\User;
use App\Models\Favorite;
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Session;
use Illuminate\Support\Collection;

class FavoritesManager
{
    protected $user;

    public function __construct(?User $user = null)
    {
        $this->user = $user;
    }

    /**
     * Get the current favorites as a collection of business names.
     */
    public function all(): Collection
    {
        if ($this->user) {
            // Fetch from DB
            return Favorite::where('user_id', $this->user->id)
                ->pluck('business_name');
        } else {
            // Fetch from session
            return collect(Session::get('favorites', []));
        }
    }

    /**
     * Add a business name to favorites.
     */
    public function add(string $businessName): void
    {
        if ($this->user) {
            Favorite::firstOrCreate([
                'user_id' => $this->user->id,
                'business_name' => $businessName,
            ]);
        } else {
            $favorites = Session::get('favorites', []);
            if (!in_array($businessName, $favorites)) {
                $favorites[] = $businessName;
                Session::put('favorites', $favorites);
            }
        }
    }

    /**
     * Remove a business name from favorites.
     */
    public function remove(string $businessName): void
    {
        if ($this->user) {
            Favorite::where('user_id', $this->user->id)
                ->where('business_name', $businessName)
                ->delete();
        } else {
            $favorites = Session::get('favorites', []);
            $favorites = array_filter($favorites, fn($name) => $name !== $businessName);
            Session::put('favorites', array_values($favorites));
        }
    }
}
```

---

## 2. FavoritesController

This controller acts as an API for the Vue frontend. Typically, you'll want endpoints to:

- List favorites (`index`)
- Add favorite (`store`)
- Remove favorite (`destroy`)

```php
<?php

namespace App\Http\Controllers;

use App\Favorites\FavoritesManager;
use Illuminate\Http\Request;
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Auth;
use Illuminate\Http\JsonResponse;

class FavoritesController extends Controller
{
    /**
     * List all favorites.
     */
    public function index(Request $request): JsonResponse
    {
        $manager = new FavoritesManager(Auth::user());
        $favorites = $manager->all();

        return response()->json([
            'favorites' => $favorites->values(),
        ]);
    }

    /**
     * Add a favorite.
     */
    public function store(Request $request): JsonResponse
    {
        $request->validate([
            'business_name' => 'required|string|max:255',
        ]);

        $manager = new FavoritesManager(Auth::user());
        $manager->add($request->input('business_name'));

        return response()->json(['status' => 'ok']);
    }

    /**
     * Remove a favorite.
     */
    public function destroy(Request $request, string $businessName): JsonResponse
    {
        $manager = new FavoritesManager(Auth::user());
        $manager->remove($businessName);

        return response()->json(['status' => 'ok']);
    }
}
```

---

## 3. Example routes (for `api.php`)

```php
Route::get('/favorites', [FavoritesController::class, 'index']);
Route::post('/favorites', [FavoritesController::class, 'store']);
Route::delete('/favorites/{businessName}', [FavoritesController::class, 'destroy']);
```

---

## 4. Notes

- If your `Favorite` model uses a different column name or has a relationship to a `Business` model, adjust accordingly.
- For guests, favorites are stored in the session, so they will be lost when the session expires or the user logs in/out.
- After login, you might want to merge session favorites into the user's account—let me know if you want help with that logic!

---

